summary:
On February 2, 2015, Apple entered into an underwriting agreement with Goldman, Sachs & Co. and Deutsche Bank Securities Inc., representing the underwriters, to issue and sell:
- $500 million in floating-rate notes due 2020;
- $1.25 billion in 1.55% notes due 2020;
- $1.25 billion in 2.15% notes due 2022;
- $1.5 billion in 2.50% notes due 2025;
- $2 billion in 3.45% notes due 2045.

The notes will be senior unsecured obligations, ranking equally with Apple’s other unsecured and unsubordinated debt. Floating-rate interest will be paid quarterly beginning May 9, 2015; fixed-rate interest will be paid semiannually beginning August 9, 2015.

The notes will be issued under Apple’s April 29, 2013 indenture and registration statement. The report includes the underwriting agreement, officer’s certificate, note forms and other supporting exhibits. Chief Financial Officer Luca Maestri signed the report on February 9, 2015.

A maternal and newborn health training intervention in Palghar District, Maharashtra, during 2019–2022 used a Training of Trainers model to develop local training capacity within the public health system. It expanded a Pune pilot project, adapted content to local health concerns, and included multiple cadres of health providers.

Eight core trainers from Pune supported the development of 32 Palghar master trainers—25 medical doctors and seven General Nurse Midwives—selected from 48 candidates. The intervention followed six steps: candidate screening, training with pre- and post-assessments, staggered induction, continuous supervision, virtual quality assessments by UK experts, and virtual refresher sessions. Training emphasized adult-learning principles, case simulations, practical demonstrations, and feedback. Intermediate-scoring trainers initially worked alongside higher-scoring peers before independently conducting training.

The master trainers trained 505 health providers over 18 months. Complete assessment data were available for 463 participants. Mean scores increased from:
- 66.1% to 79.3% for clinical knowledge;
- 36.4% to 75.5% for abdominal palpation;
- 35.4% to 74.5% for newborn resuscitation;
- 28.7% to 84.2% for eclampsia management.

These improvements were statistically significant. Interviews and observations indicated that master trainers gained clinical competence, teaching confidence, professional recognition, and motivation. Trainers reported applying clinical protocols, improving referral practices, mentoring colleagues, and adopting more participatory teaching methods.

The study emphasized careful trainer selection, extended supervised practice, continuing feedback, and a combination of in-person and virtual learning. Competing clinical and administrative responsibilities sometimes limited trainers’ availability. It recommended sustained institutional support, refresher training, teaching opportunities, accreditation, and adequate facility resources. Further evaluation is needed to assess long-term retention and application of competencies and the costs of scaling up and institutionalizing the model.

On September 12, 2017, Apple completed the issuance and sale of:
- $1 billion in 1.500% notes due September 12, 2019;
- $1 billion in 2.100% notes due September 12, 2022;
- $2 billion in 2.900% notes due September 12, 2027;
- $1 billion in 3.750% notes due September 12, 2047.

Interest is payable semiannually on March 12 and September 12, beginning March 12, 2018. The notes are senior unsecured obligations ranking equally with Apple’s other unsecured and unsubordinated debt. The underwriting agreement, dated September 5, 2017, names Goldman Sachs, Deutsche Bank Securities and Merrill Lynch as representatives of the underwriters. The report includes related agreements, note forms and legal documentation.
